---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = "From 2 Videos That Ever Worked to 160+ Ads — AI App Case Study | OKAD Agency";
const description = "How we tested 70 video ad concepts for a US AI app on Meta: 1 in 3 became winners, the best cost 35% less per customer than the account average.";
const ogImage = "/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-01.jpg";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogImage}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Reenie+Beanie&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": "From 2 Videos That Ever Worked to 160+ Ads in Four Months",
      "description": description,
      "image": "https://okad.agency/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-01.jpg",
      "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "OKAD Agency", "url": "https://okad.agency" },
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "OKAD Agency", "url": "https://okad.agency" },
      "datePublished": "2026-08-31",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://okad.agency/cases/creative-testing-ai-app-meta-ads"
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://okad.agency/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Cases", "item": "https://okad.agency/cases" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "AI App Creative Testing" }
      ]
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is creative testing?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Producing many ad variations that each change one thing — message, format or person — and letting real spend show which ones bring customers at the lowest cost." }},
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How many video ads should you test?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Enough to cover each hypothesis with at least one clean variant. Here, 70 concepts in batch 1 produced 14 winners." }},
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need to know how to run ads to work with OKAD?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. We bring research, scripts, creators, native formats and analysis; your team or media buyer runs the account." }}
      ]
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="case-designed">
<div class="w">
  <header class="top"><span class="logo">OKAD</span><span class="kick">Case study · Creative testing · client anonymised</span></header>
</div>

<!-- 1 COVER -->
<section class="cover">
  <div class="w">
    <div class="cover-grid">
      <div class="stack" style="gap:24px">
        <span class="kick">Consumer AI app · US · Meta ads</span>
        <h1>From 2 videos that ever worked <span class="cap g">to 160+ ads</span> in four months.</h1>
        <p class="lead">A fast-growing app needed ads that sell. We figured out what to say, who should say it and how to film it so it doesn't look like an ad — then let real results pick the winners.</p>
        <div class="meta">
          <div><b>April–August 2026</b><span>Pilot + 2 testing batches</span></div>
          <div><b>7 creators · 15+ formats</b><span>9:16 and 4:5 for every video</span></div>
          <div><b>Our scope</b><span>Research, strategy, scripts, casting, production, analysis</span></div>
          <div><b>Not our scope</b><span>Media buying — the client ran the account</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="collage" aria-label="Frames from the ads">
        <div class="ann hand" style="right:-6px;top:-74px">real frames from the ads<svg width="70" height="60" viewBox="0 0 70 60"><path d="M6 6 C 34 2, 56 18, 60 52"/><path d="M51 44 L60 54 L66 42"/></svg></div>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-01.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-02.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-03.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-04.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-05.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-06.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-07.jpg"></figure>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-08.jpg"></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="strip">
      <div><b>160+</b><span>video ads delivered</span></div>
      <div><b>1 in 3</b><span>test videos brought customers at the target price</span></div>
      <div><b>−35%</b><span>cheaper per customer: our best big-budget video vs the account average</span></div>
      <div><b>#1</b><span>our video got the biggest budget among ~770 ads</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="howto"><span><b>Cost per sale</b> = ad money spent to win one paying customer. Lower is better.</span><span><b>Batch</b> = one round of videos made and tested together.</span><span><b>Hook</b> = the first 1–3 seconds that make people stop scrolling.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 2 STARTING POINT -->
<section>
  <div class="w two">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q1 · Why weren't their ads working?</span>
      <h2>People had seen the same ads <span class="cap p">over and over.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">The client is an AI app that turns a few selfies into professional headshots. It spends a large monthly budget on Meta ads and wanted to roughly double it. The ads couldn't keep up: almost all of them were still images, and only one or two videos had ever worked.</p>
      <p class="pro"><b>For marketers</b>Account ran on fatigued statics; top creatives at frequency ~2; no creative diversity, no read on why winners won.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="before-after">
      <div class="box before">
        <span class="tag">Before</span>
        <ul>
          <li>Mostly static ads, all of them fatigued</li>
          <li>Only 1–2 videos had ever worked</li>
          <li>Top ads seen twice by the same people</li>
          <li>No way to tell why a winner won</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="box dark">
        <span class="tag g">The brief</span>
        <ul>
          <li>Find video winners across audiences</li>
          <li>Learn what message, format and face sell</li>
          <li>Keep a pipeline of fresh hypotheses</li>
          <li>Hand over data the team can act on</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 3 RESEARCH -->
<section>
  <div class="w">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q2 · How do you know what to say in an ad?</span>
      <h2>We don't guess. <span class="cap g">We read what customers already wrote.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">Before writing a line, we study why people buy and what stops them. Their own words become the script.</p>
      <p class="pro"><b>For marketers</b>About half of the first 70 concepts trace to a specific data point: buyer segments, review themes, objection clusters from ad comments.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sources">
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">1 · We read</span><h3>What buyers already say</h3><p>Buyer surveys, reviews, comments under the ads, past ad results and what competitors run.</p></div>
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">2 · We find</span><h3>Why they buy, and what stops them</h3><p>Who the buyer is, where they use the product, and the three doubts that kill the sale.</p></div>
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">3 · We write</span><h3>Scripts in their words</h3><p>Every concept points back to something a real customer said or did. No brainstorm guesses.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="insights">
      <div class="ins">
        <div class="quote">Buyers kept mentioning where their “before” photo came from: a car, a couch, a bathroom mirror.<small>PATTERN IN AD COMMENTS</small></div>
        <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="64" height="24" viewBox="0 0 64 24"><path d="M3 14 C 20 9, 40 16, 58 11"/><path d="M50 4 L59 11 L50 18"/></svg></div>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Split screen: car selfie on the left, AI headshot on the right" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-05.jpg"></figure>
        <p><b>Real starting points sell the result.</b> We asked creators for their actual car, couch and bathroom selfies and put them next to the output. "Left was taken in a car" became a whole series.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ins dark">
        <div class="quote">“What happens when you show up for the interview?”<small>THE #1 FEAR IN AD COMMENTS</small></div>
        <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="64" height="24" viewBox="0 0 64 24"><path d="M3 14 C 20 9, 40 16, 58 11"/><path d="M50 4 L59 11 L50 18"/></svg></div>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Same person, same face, just professionally lit" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-10.jpg"></figure>
        <p><b>People don't fear looking worse. They fear looking different.</b> So the message became "same person, same face, just professionally lit" — never "a new you".</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ins">
        <div class="quote">Most buyers are working professionals: sales, healthcare, founders, real estate — and most use the photo on LinkedIn.<small>FROM THE BUYER DATA</small></div>
        <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="64" height="24" viewBox="0 0 64 24"><path d="M3 14 C 20 9, 40 16, 58 11"/><path d="M50 4 L59 11 L50 18"/></svg></div>
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Real estate website mockup with the creator's headshot" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-11.jpg"></figure>
        <p><b>Viewers should recognise themselves in two seconds.</b> Scripts name real jobs — realtor, sales lead, founder — and show the headshot where it lives: a LinkedIn profile, a listing site.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 4 MATRIX -->
<section>
  <div class="w">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q3 · Why make 70 videos instead of 5?</span>
      <h2>Nobody can predict the winner. <span class="cap g">So we test 70 ideas and let sales decide.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">Each video checks one idea: a different reason to buy, a different style or a different person. After two weeks you know which ones bring customers.</p>
      <p class="pro"><b>For marketers</b>8 angles × 6 formats × 2 creators, 15 hypotheses, gender-swapped scripts to isolate the creator effect, 3 awareness levels, Angle_Format_Creator_Hook naming.</p>
    </div>
    <details class="more"><summary>See the 8 reasons to buy we tested</summary>
    <div class="mx-wrap">
      <table class="mx">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Reason to buy</th><th scope="col">The idea</th><th scope="col">Example hook from our scripts</th><th scope="col">Built from</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pain</td><td>The cost of an old photo</td><td class="h">"I've been putting this off for two years."</td><td>Buyers who avoid photoshoots</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Before / after</td><td>Show, don't tell</td><td class="h">"How I turned my worst photo into my best LinkedIn headshot."</td><td>Real before photos from customers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Done before your coffee cools</td><td class="h">"15 minutes. From opening the site to a new LinkedIn photo."</td><td>"It was so fast" in customer words</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Occasion</td><td>A deadline you can't move</td><td class="h">"I had 48 hours before the interview…"</td><td>Job seekers among buyers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Social proof</td><td>Results people can see</td><td class="h">"This one LinkedIn change increased profile views in 2 weeks."</td><td>Word of mouth among buyers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aspiration</td><td>Look like who you are now</td><td class="h">"POV: your LinkedIn finally looks like someone they'd want to hire."</td><td>Where buyers use the photo</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Skeptic</td><td>Answer the doubt head-on</td><td class="h">"Same person. Same face. Just professionally lit."</td><td>"Will they recognize me?"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Career stats</td><td>Research-backed urgency</td><td class="h">"Why do less qualified people get more opportunities?"</td><td>Published first-impression studies</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    </details>
    <div class="chips">
      <span class="chip"><b>15</b> hypotheses</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>70</b> concepts in batch 1</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>35 / 35</b> male / female</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>3</b> awareness levels</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>3</b> hook types per long script</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>Angle_Format_Creator_Hook</b> naming for instant analysis</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 5 FORMATS -->
<section class="dew">
  <div class="w">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q4 · Why don't your videos look like ads?</span>
      <h2>Because ads get skipped. <span class="cap p">We copy what people watch for fun — and hide the proof inside.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">An ad that looks like an ad gets skipped. So every format comes from organic TikTok and Reels — question stickers, trend memes, street-style signs, a FaceTime call — and almost every one carries the proof: the selfie next to the headshot.</p>
      <p class="pro"><b>For marketers</b>Native-first formats (UGC talking head, POV, VO + B-roll, trend memes, chat/FaceTime UI) with before/after built in; every video delivered in 9:16 and 4:5.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fmt-grid">
      <div class="ann hand" style="right:4px;top:-62px">every video in 9:16 + 4:5<svg width="70" height="60" viewBox="0 0 70 60"><path d="M6 6 C 34 2, 56 18, 60 52"/><path d="M51 44 L60 54 L66 42"/></svg></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-01.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Native: Instagram Q&amp;A sticker</span><h3>Answering a follower</h3><p>Looks like a creator replying to a question, not a brand selling.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-13.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Native: POV caption</span><h3>POV moment</h3><p>The viewer steps into the scene: checking LinkedIn before an interview.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-05.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl proof">Proof: split screen</span><h3>Left vs right</h3><p>Real selfie on the left, generated headshot on the right. No words needed.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-15.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl proof">Proof: camera roll grid</span><h3>Worst to best</h3><p>Our best video of batch 1: real bad photos, then the result.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-16.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl proof">Proof: LinkedIn mockup</span><h3>Two profiles</h3><p>Same profile, same stats, only the photo changed.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-17.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Native: explainer</span><h3>The research angle</h3><p>Studies on screen for viewers who need a reason, not a feeling.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-06.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Trend: good / better / best</span><h3>Ranking meme</h3><p>Selfie, phone portrait, AI headshot. The last one wins on its own.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-19.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Trend: handwritten quiz</span><h3>True or false</h3><p>A paper sign stops the scroll and makes people guess.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-04.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Trend: protest sign</span><h3>Written hook</h3><p>One bold fact on a sign, read in under a second.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-21.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Trend: shock &amp; flip</span><h3>Reaction, then reveal</h3><p>Seven seconds of genuine surprise, then the phone flips to the site.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-07.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Story: FaceTime with past self</span><h3>Calling April me</h3><p>One actress, two looks: the burnt-out job seeker and the one who got the offer.</p></div>
      <div class="fmt"><figure class="ph"><img alt="" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-23.jpg"></figure><span class="lbl native">Native: chat screen</span><h3>Texting a friend</h3><p>"Babe, you actually got into Google! How?" — the ad is a voice-note thread.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-row">
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">Rule 1</span><h3>Native first</h3><p>Start from a format the platform already rewards: stickers, POV, memes, chats.</p></div>
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">Rule 2</span><h3>Proof inside</h3><p>A before/after or a real profile in every video, because the transformation is the product.</p></div>
      <div class="box"><span class="tag g">Rule 3</span><h3>Real people, real rooms</h3><p>Creators shoot at home with their own old photos. Nothing looks staged or stock.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 6 ANATOMY -->
<section>
  <div class="w">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q5 · What makes a frame work?</span>
      <h2>Every part of the screen <span class="cap g">has one job.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">The overlays are designed in-house: same caption style, same before/after rings, the creator's real name on every profile. Viewers learn the visual language and read each frame in a second.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="anat">
      <div class="ann hand" style="left:70px;top:-66px;align-items:flex-start"><svg width="70" height="56" viewBox="0 0 70 56"><path d="M64 6 C 36 4, 14 18, 10 50"/><path d="M3 41 L10 52 L18 43"/></svg><span style="margin-top:-8px">read in one second</span></div>
      <div class="anat-item">
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Annotated frame: question sticker, face, caption, before and after avatars" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-01.jpg">
          <span class="pin" style="left:50%;top:16%">1</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:30%;top:40%">2</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:12%;top:61%">3</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:22%;top:79%">4</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:88%;top:66%">5</span>
        </figure>
        <ol class="notes">
          <li><span><b>Hook in the top third.</b> A native question sticker, read before the face.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Face in the centre,</b> eye-line to camera, a real room behind.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>One idea per caption,</b> white boxes like organic posts.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Proof:</b> before → after avatars in red and green rings, real name and job title.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Soft CTA</b> in the caption, never a banner.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="anat-item">
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Annotated frame: LinkedIn profile before, caption, LinkedIn profile after" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-08.jpg">
          <span class="pin" style="left:20%;top:9%">1</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:60%;top:33%">2</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:14%;top:52%">3</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:85%;top:70%">4</span>
          <span class="pin" style="left:30%;top:90%">5</span>
        </figure>
        <ol class="notes">
          <li><span><b>"Before" profile on top</b> — the old photo in a real LinkedIn layout.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Creator points at the change,</b> so the eye follows her hand.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Green highlight on the outcome:</b> "increased profile views in 2 weeks".</span></li>
          <li><span><b>"After" profile below,</b> same name, same bio, only the photo differs.</span></li>
          <li><span><b>Bottom 20% left clear</b> for the platform's buttons; key text stays inside the 4:5 crop.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 7 CASTING -->
<section>
  <div class="w">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q6 · Does it matter who's in the video?</span>
      <h2>A lot. The same words from the wrong person <span class="cap p">cost almost 2× more per customer.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">We pick people for how they sound, not how many followers they have — and we tested the same scripts with both creators to prove it.</p>
      <p class="pro"><b>For marketers</b>Creator-angle fit: weighted CPA per angle by creator; the same pain script ran at 0.45X with one creator and 0.85X with the other.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cast">
      <div class="person">
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Chris, talking head" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-26.jpg"></figure>
        <div class="stack" style="gap:6px"><span class="kick">Direct · urgent</span><h3>Chris</h3><p style="font-size:.94rem">Sounds like a friend calling you out.</p><ul><li>Pain: "I've been putting it off for 2 years"</li><li>Deadlines: "Interview in 2 days"</li><li>Recruiter fear</li></ul></div>
      </div>
      <div class="person">
        <figure class="ph"><img alt="Kaylee, speed demo" src="/images/cases/ai-app-designed/img-27.jpg"></figure>
        <div class="stack" style="gap:6px"><span class="kick">Warm · casual</span><h3>Kaylee</h3><p style="font-size:.94rem">Makes it feel easy and a little fun.</p><ul><li>Speed: "15 minutes"</li><li>Before / after transformations</li><li>Aspiration and results</li></ul></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="ann hand" style="right:20px;top:16px;align-items:flex-start">same script, different face<svg width="40" height="56" viewBox="0 0 40 56" style="margin-top:14px"><path d="M4 4 C 26 8, 32 26, 26 50"/><path d="M19 42 L26 52 L33 43"/></svg></div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 760 330" role="img" aria-label="Cost per purchase by angle for each creator">
        <g font-family="Instrument Sans, Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13">
          <text x="0" y="18" fill="var(--soft)">Cost per customer by message, relative to the account average X (lower is better)</text>
          <circle cx="8" cy="42" r="6" fill="var(--c1)"/><text x="20" y="46" fill="var(--ink)">Chris</text>
          <circle cx="88" cy="42" r="6" fill="var(--c2)"/><text x="100" y="46" fill="var(--ink)">Kaylee</text>
          <g id="ax"></g>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <p class="src-note">X = the account’s average cost per sale. Kaylee’s before/after average is pulled up by one overfunded video; her best one ran at 0.55X.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 8 SCRIPTS -->
<section class="dew">
  <div class="w two">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="q-kick">Q7 · What makes a script sell?</span>
      <h2>Short, personal, <span class="cap g">in the customer's own words.</span></h2>
      <p class="lead">This product is bought on impulse by busy professionals. So scripts hit a feeling in the first second, prove it with a real photo, and finish in under 20 seconds.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="hooks">
        <div class="box dark"><span class="tag g dewtag">Openings that sold</span>
          <ul class="hook-list">
            <li>"Interview in 2 days. No headshot." <span>0.4X</span></li>
            <li>"Recruiters notice your photo first." <span>0.45X</span></li>
            <li>"From my worst selfies to my best headshot." <span>0.55X</span></li>
            <li>"My LinkedIn views tripled." <span>0.65X</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box plain"><span class="tag">Openings we dropped</span>
          <ul class="hook-list">
            <li>"Recruiters judge you in 100 ms." <span>0 sales</span></li>
            <li>"Your career is not a coincidence." <span>3.9X</span></li>
            <li>"Does it actually look like you?" <span>1.2X</span></li>
            <li>Anything longer than 20 s <span>1.25X</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
